%% UC-46 — Re-identify a participant
%%
%% This is the only path from a subject code back to a person. The workflow
%% exists to make that deliberate and visible, NOT to make it hard: in practice
%% it takes a couple of minutes and does not slow down a real emergency.
%%
%% Three things are worth reading off this diagram:
%%   - the four-eyes rule is a database TRIGGER, so it cannot be bypassed by a
%%     change to application code;
%%   - the grant is time-limited AND field-limited — asking for a phone number
%%     does not also return an address;
%%   - there is no bulk reveal and no endpoint that accepts a list of subject
%%     codes. That absence is what makes "quietly de-anonymise the cohort" not
%%     something the software can do, regardless of who is logged in.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor IM as Identity Manager
    actor MO as Monitor (a different person)
    participant UI as Admin Portal
    participant I as identity-service (:3002 public)
    participant PG as identity-db (PostgreSQL)
    participant SMTP as SMTP relay
    actor DPO as Data Protection Officer

    Note over IM,UI: A `researcher` holding any identity role is refused at<br/>runtime (403 role_separation_violation). The person who<br/>analyses the pseudonymous data cannot resolve it.

    IM->>UI: Raise a request
    UI->>I: POST /v1/studies/:id/reidentification-requests<br/>{ subjectCode, legalBasis, reason, fieldsRequested }
    I->>I: requireIdentityRole + assignment check<br/>(a role says WHAT, an assignment says WHERE)
    I->>PG: INSERT reidentification_requests (requested_by = caller)
    Note over PG: requested_by is what the trigger compares against.<br/>It was once bound to a missing placeholder and silently<br/>NULL — which disabled BOTH the four-eyes rule and the<br/>reveal requester check. Caught by the self-approval test.
    I->>PG: audit: create_reidentification_request (write)
    I-->>UI: 201 { id, status: pending }

    IM->>UI: Approve own request
    UI->>I: POST /v1/reidentification-requests/:id/decide { approved }
    I->>PG: INSERT reidentification_approvals
    PG--xI: enforce_four_eyes() raises
    I-->>UI: 409 — the requester may not approve

    MO->>UI: Approve
    UI->>I: POST /v1/reidentification-requests/:id/decide { approved }
    I->>PG: INSERT approval, set status=approved,<br/>reveal_expires_at = now() + revealTtlMinutes
    I-->>UI: 200

    IM->>UI: Reveal
    UI->>I: GET /v1/reidentification-requests/:id/reveal
    I->>PG: check status, window, and that the caller is the requester
    I->>PG: UPDATE … SET reveal_count = reveal_count + 1 RETURNING
    Note over PG: Counted by the database, not in JS: two concurrent<br/>reveals would otherwise report the same number.
    PG-->>I: wrapped DEK + the requested ciphertext columns
    I->>I: unwrap DEK, decrypt ONLY fieldsRequested
    I->>PG: audit: reveal — never deduplicated, records field NAMES
    I->>SMTP: alert — subject code, actor, legal basis, field names
    SMTP-->>DPO: on EVERY reveal, not on a threshold
    Note over I,DPO: The alert says that someone was identified.<br/>It never carries the values.
    I-->>UI: { subjectCode, fields, revealCount, expiresAt }

    opt approval granted in error
        MO->>UI: Revoke
        UI->>I: POST /v1/reidentification-requests/:id/revoke
        I->>PG: status=revoked — the window closes immediately
        Note over MO,I: The alternative was waiting out the TTL, which is<br/>the wrong answer under the time pressure that<br/>produces a mistaken approval in the first place.
    end
